  "notes": "West leads the ♥J against 4♠. West's heart opening lead goes to the ♥A. East returns a heart, South winning the ♥K. The contract will be in danger only if West has the ♣A and East has the ♦K.\n\nIf this is the case, South's best chance is to draw trump ending in the closed hand and play a low club towards the dummy. If the ♣10 is with West and the ♣Q is with East, West must duck. Dummy's ♣9 will force the ♣Q. East will be unable to attack diamonds from his side of the table hand South will be able to build a club discard for his diamond loser.\n\nThis line of play will look silly if the cards are distributed as above. West will win the ♣Q and play a diamond through dummy. All South had to do was lead clubs towards the ♣K.\n\nThis East hand is impossible, however. East is a passed hand. He is marked with the ♥A and ♥Q from the opening lead. If East really has the ♣A he cannot have the ♦K as well or he would have opened the bidding.\n\nAny time that leading a club towards the ♣K works, the diamond finesse will also work. It is thus correct to lead a small club towards the dummy intending to play the ♣9.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
